@Steve L
Tallying T's Tarnishes Thee!

I got the theme; I thought it was cute;  I didn't count T's in the puzzle or theme phrases.  It's all fine.

@Spencer

I did get a bit confused part way through, and thought I'd made a mistake, but couldn't see what I'd gotten wrong.
I think if the clue for 39A had hinted at, or referred to, the initial letters of the other answers, that'd have reduced any confusion.

@sotto voce 

Totally off-topic, unasked-for opinion:  Nuno Bettencourt’s genetics (or skin care regimen) aside, I really hate that song. 

A friend once asked my wife and me to sing it at here wedding. I was unfamiliar with the song, and when I first heard it, and then looked closely at the lyrics, I thought, SRSLY? It was admittedly fun to sing, and I’m a fan of two-part rock n roll harmony, but those words (ha ha)… it’s a horn dog pressuring the object of his desire into physical affection, and it struck me as singularly inappropriate for a wedding, but, hey, the bride asked. (I’ll add that they’re still happily married some 20 years later, so, yay!)

@Jane Wheelaghan
Re Yesterday. Me too. I gave up and pressed Reveal. Maths and I are never going to get on.
But yes, I did know GAIUS.

@Jane Wheelaghan

Thank you for the empathy and compassion! 

But you can rest easy; I think mine is worse. After all, many, many people are mathematically challenged, even traumatized by it, whereas being 63 years old and not knowing Caesar's full name is, in my eyes, simply not normal. :-)

@Andrzej

Desperate puzzles sometimes call for desperate measures…
My best defense is I can find lots of citations for it, and but it is more in archaic usage. It has been used 8 times in previous NYT puzzles which is my best justification. It did give me pause, though, so fair nit.

@Ιασων

The clue/fill is even more problematic when you consider that the "world" part began only a few days later with the declaration of war by Britain and France just a few days after the German invasion of Poland and even before Russia piled on.

"Main decade..." would have headed off the justified grumbling.

@Andrzej

I hate to say it, but I think part of it might be Americans being casually and ignorantly self centered. I know, I know, it barely ever happens. But we didn't enter the war until the 40s and I think, while we know it started in the 30s, people might unintentionally kind of hand wave that away with the idea that, "well, it was mostly the 40s anyway". Not too say that everyone does, but I think there's kind of a "30s=depression, 40s=WWII, 50s=baby boom" simplicity in the way we were taught this stuff in school. 

I'm sorry about your great grandfather ❤️

@Andrzej
@Wyra

Just to add a note about American blind spot for anything that doesn't affect them, I remember going back to the newspapers and news magazines in that era, and for much of early 1940, they called it "The Phony War".  

Nothing much was happening, you see, that mattered to us.

@Chase

England, France, and Germany to a lesser extent had possessions all over the world at the time, and fighting took place there, too - in Africa, for example. Also, the USSR spanned two continents, and it joined the war in 1939 already.

(And yes, Europe is part of the world, and the European country of Germany drew much of the rest of Earth into the war, which began in 1939)

@Ιασων
And even though the US had not declared WAR, we were busily supplying arms and other assistance, and the military were beefing up their officer corps.  
My dad, happy as an Army First Sergeant in the Artillery, was pressed to apply to OCS.  He was still in training in December, 1941; all candidates were sent home and told "get your affairs in order, as you will have no further leave for the duration."  So he went home to Atlanta and married our mother.

@Gareth
As someone who grew up in Pittsburgh, I can look at a map of Britain and ascertain that Wales is entirely in the southern half and the western half of the island.
*shrug* SW, to me.

@Nancy J. And all Protesting Citizens of The UK

I was spared that angst because the W was in square 37 already, meaning it had to be WELSH.  Fleetingly, "This means trouble" went through my head, but at least I didn't have a crisis over the insult dealt to Cornwall, etc.
We often notice that the crew at the NYT doesn't bother to double check the clues they revise...

@Mean Old Lady

Problems with WELSH?  Who me?  I didn't know enough to know that would be controversial.

As for *my* comment, now that I had some time to investigate, I guess I should have known GAIUS without any crosses.  I see now that it's been in the puzzle four times since I started solving.  Good thing the crosses made sure I didn't need it today, and hopefully I'll remember it when they're less friendly.

@SP
Not sure if you saw my response...(my excuse, rather)...re repeating myself.
My extra 15 years, it seems, reveal "signs of normal aging"  --ever more tiresome!

@Nancy

Kitty Carlisle.  I'm glad you brought her up.  She's in my most distant memories, entirely from To Tell the Truth, because I didn't make it too see very much New York theater.

It was interesting to find out more about her life before I was aware.  I was also stunned to find out she lived until 2007.

